    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. To go a bit deeper into the mTOR mechanism, how do the different amino acid transporters facilitate this? Also, are there any specific timing or threshold levels for leucine intake that would make the mTOR activation more efficient for a client? I want to make sure the dosing is optimized for the signaling response.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Amino acid transporters, like LAT1, are crucial because they facilitate the cellular uptake of essential amino acids, including leucine, into muscle cells where mTOR resides. Adequate transport ensures a high concentration gradient of leucine reaches the signaling machinery. For optimal mTOR activation, a threshold dose of leucine, often cited around 2.5–3 grams per meal, is generally necessary to achieve a significant signaling spike. Spacing these doses strategically around training can help maximize the duration and efficacy of the anabolic response.",
      "source": "target",
